[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Immutability and Value Objects in Object-Oriented Programming"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern Object-Oriented Programming, managing state is one of the most complex challenges. Mutable state introduces side effects, making systems difficult to reason about, test, and scale. Immutability, combined with the concept of Value Objects, provides a robust paradigm to eliminate these hidden bugs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is an Immutable Object?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An immutable object is an object whose internal state cannot be modified after it is constructed. Once you instantiate the object, its properties are locked for its entire lifecycle. If you need to change a value, you do not mutate the existing instance; instead, you create a new instance with the updated state."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Value Objects"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike Entities, which are defined by a unique identity (like a User ID or Account Number), Value Objects are defined entirely by their attributes. Two Value Objects with the same attribute values are considered equal. Crucially, Value Objects should always be immutable."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Implementing a Money Value Object"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class Money {
    private final decimal amount;
    private final string currency;

    public Money(decimal amount, string currency) {
        if (amount < 0) {
            throw new ArgumentException("Amount cannot be negative");
        }
        this.amount = amount;
        this.currency = currency;
    }

    public decimal getAmount() { return amount; }
    public string getCurrency() { return currency; }

    public Money add(Money other) {
        if (!this.currency.equals(other.currency)) {
            throw new ArgumentException("Currencies must match");
        }
        return new Money(this.amount + other.amount, this.currency);
    }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Rules for Designing Value Objects"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Make all fields private and final (or read-only)."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not provide setter methods."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Validate all inputs during construction to ensure the object is always in a valid state."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Implement structural equality so two objects with identical values evaluate as equal."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Immutability"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Thread Safety: Immutable objects can be shared freely across multiple threads without synchronization locks."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Predictability: Functions cannot unexpectedly alter the state of passed arguments."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Simplified Debugging: Time-travel debugging and caching become trivial because objects never change underneath you."}]
